#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check the reply the user is about to read, at the moment it is finished.

WHY THIS EXISTS. `wf-plain-guard` checks a QUESTION, and only its words. Nothing
checked a REPORT, which is where the register actually reaches the user. MEASURED
2026-09-07: a run closed with twenty lines of stacked-clause narration whose one real
ask sat on the last line, and the user answered "You're not making any sense at all."
The reply to THAT opened with "You're right." — the sycophantic-opener pattern
documented for Opus at launch: agreement signal first, correction reframed as an
addition.

WHY A `Stop` HOOK AND NOT MORE PROSE. The rule already existed in three places —
operating-principles item 11, `/org` rung 13, and SKILL.md principle 8b. All three are
loaded at the head of a context and compete with everything newer as the session grows.
That is the same decay the 2026-08-05 CLAUDE.md directive names. A hook does not decay:
`Stop` fires once per turn, at full strength, on turn 2 and on turn 200 alike, and it
receives `last_assistant_message` — the finished text — so it reads what the user is
about to read rather than a description of it.

WHAT IT CHECKS. Five things, all mechanical, all about the SHAPE of the reply:

  1. A sycophantic or retracting OPENER. Whole-phrase, anchored to the first sentence.
     This is the one hard block. "You're absolutely right" carries no information; it
     costs the user a line and it is the observable surface of flip-flopping under
     pushback, which is measurably likelier exactly when the user has pushed back.
  2. A word the USER HAS NEVER USED. The banned list in `references/plain-output.md`,
     scoped by the session transcript: a term is a hit only when the user has not typed
     it themselves. This is the rule "words the reader already owns", stated so that it
     can be executed. It is why the list can be applied to reports at all — a flat list
     would fire on every reply in a repository whose subject IS handbooks and tiers, and
     a guard that fires on everything is a guard that gets turned off.
  3. A BURIED ASK — a long reply whose only question sits in the last quarter.
  4. A HAND-BACK — "one thing left for you", "you'll need to run", "over to you" — with
     no named reason the model could not do it itself. BLOCKS. Added 2026-09-07 on the
     user's directive: *"you won't communicate a problem to me like it's an issue that
     holds up the works, when you can actually start addressing the problem and solve
     it."* Measured the same day: a run finished eight of eight items, found and fixed
     eleven more, then closed by handing the user a command to type — which turned out
     to be a step no executor on this host could run, i.e. a defect in the procedure
     rather than a chore for a human. A hand-back is legitimate ONLY when something
     named refuses (a classifier, a missing credential, a rule that says ask), and the
     check passes as soon as the reply says which. It never asks whether the reason is
     good; it asks whether one is given.
  5. FINDINGS WITH NO DISPOSITION. A reply that names defects and never says what
     happened to any of them reads as a problem list when it is a done list. Same
     directive: *"you will never communicate to me a problem like it's still a problem
     when it's already taken care of."* Every finding carries its outcome in the same
     breath — fixed, blocked by <named thing>, or your call because <reason>. NUDGE
     rather than block, because the pairing of a specific finding to its specific
     disposition is a judgment, and only the total absence of any disposition is
     mechanical.

  6. A MESSAGE THAT REPEATS ONE ALREADY SENT THIS TURN. Reports the overlap as a
     NUMBER and rules on nothing. MEASURED 2026-09-08: a session sent three consecutive
     messages restating one list, then two more restating one paragraph, and every check
     that existed passed all five because each was well-formed ON ITS OWN. The defect
     only exists BETWEEN messages. The user could not tell the third message was the
     first one again until he had finished reading it.

     This is arithmetic over a generic English stoplist, not a vocabulary check: its
     whole configuration is one float, and covering a new phrasing requires nothing,
     because two accounts of one list share their nouns however the sentences are
     rewritten. It NEVER blocks. A blocked recap is a ruling on whether a restatement
     was worth sending, which is a judgment; a stated overlap is a fact the model can
     act on or explain.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CHECK. Register, length, and whether the prose is overbuilt. Those are
the coffee test (`plain-output.md` § The test), a human judgment. A checker scoring
prose quality would fire on everything, which is failure mode 2 above.

WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT, and it belongs in this list because the absence is a
decision rather than an oversight. Pronoun ambiguity — "it" meaning both the worker
agent and the test suite in one sentence, the failure the user called "talking in
circles" — is NOT checked here. Nor is the one-claim-per-sentence discipline that GSAR
names as the defeater of any grounding partition. Neither is computable from a
transcript, and every mechanical approximation of either is a curated word list that
grows forever and fires on good prose; a first attempt at the pronoun check false-fired
on a clean reply within twenty minutes of shipping. Both rules live in
`output-styles/plain-speak.md`, which is read at full strength on every turn, and they
stay there. An honestly empty slot beats a checker that has to be apologised for.

BLOCK VS NUDGE. An opener blocks: `decision: block` returns the reply to the model with
one instruction, and the user never sees the draft. Everything else returns
`additionalContext` — the turn ends, the user reads the reply, and the model carries the
note into the next one. Blocking a whole turn over a buried question would cost more
than the question did.

LOOP SAFETY. `stop_hook_active` true means this hook already blocked once this turn;
it passes through unconditionally. Combined with the host's 8-continuation cap, a
disagreement between guard and model ends in the user's favour rather than in a spin.

NO TERM IS WRITTEN HERE. The banned list lives in `references/plain-output.md` and is
parsed from it, exactly as `wf-plain-guard` does, so adding a word stays a one-line edit
to the reference (SKILL.md Principle 9a). The OPENERS list is written here, because it
is a property of the reply's first sentence rather than of the vocabulary, and
`plain-output.md` § Openers names it and points here.

EXIT CODES: 0 always. A verdict travels as JSON on stdout, never as an exit code: a
guard bug must never wedge a session, and exit 2 on `Stop` is indistinguishable from a
crash.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import sys

_REF = os.path.join("references", "plain-output.md")

_LIST_RE = re.compile(
    r"\*\*Banned from question text and from every option label\*\*(.*?)(?:\n\n)", re.S)

# Openers that carry no information. Anchored to the reply's opening — the same phrase
# mid-paragraph is usually a real concession with a reason attached, and this guard has
# no way to tell a reasoned one from a reflex except by position.
OPENERS = (
    "you're absolutely right",
    "you are absolutely right",
    "you're right",
    "you are right",
    "you're correct",
    "you are correct",
    "good catch",
    "great catch",
    "great question",
    "excellent question",
    "that's a great point",
    "that's a fair point",
    "you make a good point",
    "you raise a good point",
    "i apologize",
    "i apologise",
    "my apologies",
    "i was wrong",
    "i was completely wrong",
    "you're right to",
    "now that you mention it",
    "good point",
)

# How much of the reply counts as "the opener". One sentence, generously measured.
_OPENER_WINDOW = 120

# CLAUSE 2 — handing work back. Phrase-matched, and the bar is deliberately narrow:
# an explicit transfer of an action to the reader. "Say the word and I'll commit" is
# NOT here, because it offers to do the work rather than assigning it.
_HANDBACK = (
    "left for you", "left to you", "one thing left", "needs your hand",
    "over to you", "you'll need to", "you will need to", "you need to run",
    "for you to run", "for you to do", "requires you to", "up to you to",
    "waiting on you", "i'll leave that to you", "that's yours to",
)
# A named reason the model could not act. Presence of ANY of these clears clause 2 —
# the check asks whether a reason is given, never whether it is a good one. Judging
# the reason would be a taste call, and a guard that makes taste calls gets disabled.
_BLOCKED_BY = (
    "refused", "refuses", "denied", "denies", "blocked by", "not permitted",
    "no permission", "classifier", "credential", "you haven't asked",
    "have not asked", "only you can", "your call", "needs your approval",
    "requires approval", "cannot reach", "no access", "unavailable",
)
# CLAUSE 1 — a defect named with no outcome attached anywhere in the reply.
_FINDING = (
    "defect", "bug", "broken", "is missing", "was missing", "no caller",
    "nothing calls", "stale", "wrong", "failed", "failing", "gap",
    "does not exist", "doesn't exist", "unproven", "vacuous",
)
_DISPOSITION = (
    "fixed", "fixes", "corrected", "closed", "landed", "added", "removed",
    "repaired", "resolved", "done", "already", "now ", "no longer",
    "blocked by", "your call", "refused", "denied",
)

# CLAUSE 6 — how much of this message is a message already sent this turn. Generic
# English only: this is the machinery for measuring overlap, NOT a list of things to
# look for, so it never grows in response to a new failure.
_REPEAT_MIN_WORDS = 12     # below this there is not enough content to compare
_REPEAT_REPORT = 0.55      # Jaccard, so both messages must be near the same size AND
                           # the same content. Calibrated against the two measured
                           # duplicates (0.60) and a real corpus of 400 replies.
_STOPWORDS = frozenset((
    "the a an and or but so it its is was were are be been being to of in on at for "
    "with from that this these those i you he she they we my your not no yes as by if "
    "then than there here what which who when how all any can could would should will "
    "just now still only more most one two three do does did done have has had").split())

_SYSTEM_BLOCK = re.compile(r"<system-reminder>.*?</system-reminder>", re.S)
_FENCE = re.compile(r"```.*?```", re.S)


def _note(msg):
    sys.stderr.write("wf-speak-guard: " + msg + "\n")


def _read_payload():
    try:
        return json.loads(sys.stdin.read() or "{}")
    except (ValueError, OSError):
        return None


def _ref_path(root):
    """The shipped reference, resolved beside this script first — see wf-plain-guard."""
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    for cand in (os.path.join(os.path.dirname(here), _REF),
                 os.path.join(root, _REF)):
        if os.path.exists(cand):
            return cand
    return None


def parse_terms(text):
    """The banned terms, read from the reference's own list paragraph.

    [] means could-not-parse, which passes through. Refusing a reply because a
    reference moved would wedge a session over the guard's own bookkeeping."""
    m = _LIST_RE.search(text)
    if not m:
        return []
    terms = []
    for tok in re.findall(r"`([^`]+)`", m.group(1)):
        tok = tok.strip().lower()
        if tok and re.match(r"^[a-z][a-z -]*$", tok):
            terms.append(tok)
    return sorted(set(terms), key=len, reverse=True)


def user_words(transcript_path):
    """Everything the user has typed this session, lowercased, as one string.

    Skips meta injections and tool results: neither is the user speaking, and counting
    a system-reminder's vocabulary as the user's own would license every term in this
    distribution the moment a hook mentioned one.

    Returns None when the transcript cannot be read, which DISABLES the vocabulary
    check rather than firing it — an unreadable transcript means every term looks
    unfamiliar, and blocking on that would be the guard inventing a finding."""
    if not transcript_path or not os.path.exists(transcript_path):
        return None
    out = []
    try:
        with open(transcript_path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                try:
                    d = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if d.get("type") != "user" or d.get("isMeta") or d.get("toolUseResult"):
                    continue
                content = (d.get("message") or {}).get("content")
                if isinstance(content, str):
                    out.append(content)
                elif isinstance(content, list):
                    for blk in content:
                        if isinstance(blk, dict) and blk.get("type") == "text":
                            out.append(str(blk.get("text") or ""))
    except OSError:
        return None
    return _SYSTEM_BLOCK.sub(" ", "\n".join(out)).lower()


def opener_hit(message):
    """The opening phrase, when it is one of OPENERS. Whole-phrase, position-anchored."""
    head = message.lstrip().lower()[:_OPENER_WINDOW]
    head = head.lstrip("*_#- \t")
    for phrase in OPENERS:
        if re.match(r"^\s*" + re.escape(phrase) + r"(?![\w'])", head):
            return phrase
    return None


def unowned_terms(message, terms, owned):
    """Banned terms in the reply that the user has never typed.

    `owned is None` means the transcript was unreadable — see user_words."""
    if owned is None or not terms:
        return []
    prose = _FENCE.sub(" ", message).lower()
    found = []
    for term in terms:
        pat = r"(?<![\w-])" + re.escape(term) + r"(?![\w-])"
        if re.search(pat, prose) and not re.search(pat, owned):
            found.append(term)
    return found


def buried_ask(message):
    """True when a long reply hides its only question at the bottom.

    The threshold is lines of prose, not characters: a reply that fits on a screen has
    no room to bury anything, and this fires on the shape the user named — twenty lines
    of narration with "say go and I'll start it" on the last one."""
    prose = _FENCE.sub("", message)
    lines = [ln for ln in prose.splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    if len(lines) < 12:
        return False
    marks = [i for i, ln in enumerate(lines) if "?" in ln]
    if not marks:
        return False
    return min(marks) >= int(len(lines) * 0.75)


def handback_hit(message):
    """(phrase, True) when work is assigned to the reader with no reason given.

    Fenced blocks are stripped first: a command shown inside a fence is often the
    thing the model just RAN, and matching prose inside code would fire on evidence.
    """
    prose = _FENCE.sub("", message).lower()
    hit = next((ph for ph in _HANDBACK if ph in prose), None)
    if not hit:
        return None
    if any(r in prose for r in _BLOCKED_BY):
        return None
    return hit


def findings_without_disposition(message):
    """True when a reply names defects and states no outcome for anything.

    Deliberately coarse. It cannot pair a finding to its own disposition — that is a
    judgment — so it fires only on the total absence, which is the shape the user
    named: a reply that reads as a list of problems when the work is already done.
    """
    prose = _FENCE.sub("", message).lower()
    if not any(f in prose for f in _FINDING):
        return False
    return not any(d in prose for d in _DISPOSITION)


def prior_assistant_turn(transcript_path):
    """Assistant messages already sent since the user's last message, oldest first.

    The final row is DROPPED by the caller: at `Stop` the transcript already holds the
    reply being judged, so keeping it would compare a message to itself and score 100%.
    Measured 2026-09-08, by an earlier form of this check blocking the first reply it
    ever ran on."""
    if not transcript_path or not os.path.exists(transcript_path):
        return []
    out = []
    try:
        with open(transcript_path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                try:
                    d = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                kind = d.get("type")
                if kind == "user" and not d.get("isMeta") and not d.get("toolUseResult"):
                    out = []
                    continue
                if kind != "assistant":
                    continue
                c = (d.get("message") or {}).get("content")
                txt = ""
                if isinstance(c, str):
                    txt = c
                elif isinstance(c, list):
                    txt = "\n".join(str(b.get("text") or "") for b in c
                                    if isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("type") == "text")
                if txt.strip():
                    out.append(txt)
    except OSError:
        return []
    return out


def _content(text):
    return {w for w in re.findall(r"[a-z][a-z'-]+", text.lower())
            if w not in _STOPWORDS and len(w) > 2}


def repeat_overlap(message, prior):
    """Share of this message's content words already sent this turn, or 0.0.

    Reports a number. It does not decide whether restating was warranted — sometimes it
    is — and that is why this returns a measurement and the caller never blocks on it."""
    here = _content(_FENCE.sub("", message))
    if len(here) < _REPEAT_MIN_WORDS:
        return 0.0
    best = 0.0
    for earlier in prior:
        there = _content(_FENCE.sub("", earlier))
        if len(there) < _REPEAT_MIN_WORDS:
            continue
        # JACCARD, not overlap-over-the-smaller. Dividing by the smaller set scores a
        # short closing summary of a long earlier message at ~100%, because a subset is
        # wholly contained in its superset — and summarising at the end of a turn is a
        # legitimate act, not a repeat. Measured over 400 real replies, the min()
        # denominator fired 24 times and the samples were progress notes, not
        # restatement. Jaccard divides by the union, so it only reaches the threshold
        # when the two messages are near the same SIZE as well as the same content,
        # which is what "you said this already" actually looks like.
        union = len(here | there)
        if union:
            best = max(best, len(here & there) / float(union))
    return best if best >= _REPEAT_REPORT else 0.0


def main():
    payload = _read_payload()
    if payload is None:
        _note("could not verify — stdin is not a JSON payload; passing through")
        return 0

    # Already blocked once this turn. Never block again: the model and the guard
    # disagreeing is not worth a spin the user has to interrupt.
    if payload.get("stop_hook_active"):
        return 0

    message = payload.get("last_assistant_message") or ""
    if not message.strip():
        return 0

    event = payload.get("hook_event_name") or "Stop"

    phrase = opener_hit(message)
    if phrase:
        print(json.dumps({
            "decision": "block",
            "reason": (
                "Your reply opens with \"%s\". Delete that sentence and start with what "
                "is true or what you did. Agreement carries no information, and opening "
                "with it reads as folding rather than as thinking. If you changed your "
                "mind, say what changed it in one sentence, then move on — no apology, "
                "no tally of the mistake." % phrase),
        }))
        return 0

    handback = handback_hit(message)
    if handback:
        print(json.dumps({
            "decision": "block",
            "reason": (
                "Your reply hands work to the user (\"%s\") without naming anything that "
                "stopped you. Do it. If something genuinely refuses you — a permission "
                "classifier, a missing credential, a rule that says ask — say which, in "
                "one line, and finish everything else. A step no agent here can run is a "
                "defect in the step, not a chore for the reader." % handback),
        }))
        return 0

    root = payload.get("cwd") or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd()
    ref = os.environ.get("WF_SPEAK_GUARD_REF") or _ref_path(root)
    terms = []
    if ref and os.path.exists(ref):
        try:
            with open(ref, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                terms = parse_terms(fh.read())
        except OSError as exc:
            _note("could not read %s — %s; vocabulary check skipped" % (ref, exc))

    owned = user_words(payload.get("transcript_path"))
    unowned = unowned_terms(message, terms, owned)
    buried = buried_ask(message)
    undisposed = findings_without_disposition(message)

    overlap = repeat_overlap(
        message, prior_assistant_turn(payload.get("transcript_path"))[:-1])

    notes = []
    if overlap:
        notes.append(
            "This message shares %d%% of its content words with one you already sent "
            "this turn. If it is the same account again, send one message instead of "
            "two — the reader cannot tell it is a repeat until they have finished "
            "reading it. If it genuinely adds something, lead with the part that is new."
            % int(overlap * 100))
    if undisposed:
        notes.append(
            "This reply names problems and never says what happened to any of them. "
            "Every finding carries its outcome in the same breath — fixed, blocked by "
            "<the named thing>, or the reader's call because <reason>. A finding "
            "reported without one reads as still open when it is already closed.")
    if unowned:
        notes.append(
            "The user has not used %s in this session, and this reply did. Say it in "
            "words they already own, or name the thing instead of the machinery."
            % " / ".join('"%s"' % t for t in unowned))
    if buried:
        notes.append(
            "The only question in this reply is in its last quarter. The reader had to "
            "get through everything else to find what you needed from them. Put the ask "
            "first next time, or decide it yourself and say what you decided.")
    if not notes:
        return 0

    print(json.dumps({
        "hookSpecificOutput": {
            "hookEventName": event,
            "additionalContext": " ".join(notes),
        }
    }))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except Exception as exc:  # a guard bug must never wedge a session
        _note("could not verify — %s: %s; passing through" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
        sys.exit(0)
